insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 1, 'The Associate tier and this tier answer different questions about a file. Which statement captures the difference?', '["Reading a file asks whether it is believable, and importing a file asks whether the vendor delivered on schedule.","Reading a file asks what unit it is in, and importing a file asks how many samples it holds.","Reading a file asks whether it is believable, and importing a file asks what it becomes inside the project.","Reading a file and importing a file ask the same question, with import adding a database write at the end."]'::jsonb, 2, 'QC output goes to a person who can apply judgement to it, while import output goes to software that assumes metres and never asks. That difference in consumer is why the two are separate skills.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 2, 'The import pipeline makes four decisions about every file. Which of these is NOT one of them?', '["Whether the well''s surface location falls inside the licence block.","The depth unit, read from the depth curve''s declaration and converted.","The curve kinds, assigned so a downstream application can ask for the gamma ray by kind.","The step uniformity of the converted depth column, returned as a verdict."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The four decisions are the depth unit, the curve units, the curve kinds and the step uniformity. Surface coordinates are absent from most LAS files and stay manual, and no licence check happens at import.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 3, 'At which point in the pipeline is the depth column converted from feet to metres?', '["In the parser, as the data section is tokenised.","At registration, as the log rows are written.","In the parser for the depth column and at registration for every other curve.","In the prepare stage, between parsing and registration."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Parse produces structure and nothing else, prepare owns every conversion, and register adds identity and persistence with no arithmetic at all.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 4, 'Why is the parser forbidden from converting anything, even when the file''s depth unit is unambiguous?', '["Because conversion is slow and the parser sits on the critical path for large files.","Because the parser''s output is validated against published reference goldens that pin raw parse fidelity.","Because the parser has no access to the factor table at the point where it reads the data section.","Because converting inside the parser would give the wrong answer for files that are already in metres."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A parser that converted could no longer be checked against its goldens, which would cost the pipeline the one stage whose correctness can be demonstrated against an outside authority.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 5, 'A file declares its depth curve in a unit string the importer does not recognise. Where does the import stop?', '["At registration, when the well record fails a unit constraint.","In the parser, when the tokeniser meets the unrecognised unit.","Nowhere. The depth column passes through with a warning and its converted flag left false.","In prepare, at the factor lookup and before any conversion is attempted, with a message naming the unit and pointing at the ~Curve section."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The lookup fails first, so nothing is converted. Passing the column through with a warning is the tempting behaviour and the dangerous one, since a warning is read by whoever is watching at the time while the well outlives them.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 6, 'What does the importer use to decide whether a given curve needs converting?', '["The curve''s declared unit string alone, looked up in a fixed table.","The curve''s mnemonic, since the mnemonic determines what the curve measures.","A dimensional analysis of the declared unit, so that any unit carrying a length is converted.","The file''s depth unit, applied to every curve in the file at once."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Conversion is a table lookup on the unit string. Assuming a dimensional analysis is the classic error, since a length unit absent from the table passes through unconverted.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 7, 'What does the importer use to decide a curve''s kind?', '["The declared unit, since GAPI identifies a gamma ray and G/C3 identifies a density.","The unit and the mnemonic together, with the unit breaking ties between candidates.","The mnemonic alone, upper-cased and matched against fixed name lists.","The curve''s position in the file, counting outward from the index curve."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Kind recognition never consults the unit. The index curve is the one exception to mnemonic matching, and it is stamped as the depth curve because it comes first.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 8, 'feet_20.las declares five curves and the pipeline reports four recognised curve kinds. Why four?', '["One of the five mnemonics is a vendor spelling the importer''s name lists do not carry.","The neutron curve arrives in V/V, which the kind lists do not cover.","DEPT is stamped as the index curve because it comes first, so it is excluded from the count of recognised value curves.","The count reports distinct kinds, and two of the five curves happen to share one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The index curve is not a measurement, so it does not count among the recognised kinds. Answering five is the classic error and it comes from treating the depth column as a curve like the others.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 9, 'feet_20.las runs from 4900 ft to 5200 ft at a 2 ft step. How many depth samples should the parser report?', '["150, because 300 ft of range divided by a 2 ft step gives 150 increments and the count follows the increments.","151, because 300 ft of range at a 2 ft step gives 150 increments and both ends are included.","300, because the count follows the range in feet rather than the step that was used to sample it.","152, because the count includes a header row above the data block."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The count is (5200 - 4900) / 2 + 1 = 151, and the parser reports 151. Answering 150 is the off-by-one that comes from forgetting the inclusive end.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 10, 'Which line describes feet_20.las as it arrives, before any conversion?', '["Depth unit F, start 4900 ft, stop 5200 ft, step 2 ft, 151 samples.","Depth unit F, start 4900 ft, stop 5200 ft, step 2 ft, 301 samples.","Depth unit M, start 4900 m, stop 5200 m, step 2 m, 151 samples.","Depth unit F, start 1493.52001953125 ft, stop 1584.9599609375 ft, step 2 ft, 151 samples."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Those are the native header facts, every depth in feet. The last option is the classic confusion, since 1493.52001953125 m is a converted depth in metres and is never a native value in feet.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 11, 'Which curves of feet_20.las does the pipeline convert?', '["DEPT only, since the depth column is the only place a length appears.","DEPT, GR and DT, since all three arrive in units the importer rewrites.","All five, since the file as a whole is denominated in feet.","DEPT and DT, giving a converted count of 2."]'::jsonb, 3, 'DEPT goes from F to M and DT goes from US/F to US/M, so the graded count is 2. Answering 1 is the classic error and it comes from treating unit trouble as a property of the depth column.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 12, 'GR, RHOB and NPHI pass through the import of feet_20.las unchanged. What do their units have in common?', '["They are all dimensionless quantities, so there is no conversion that could meaningfully apply to any of them.","GAPI, G/C3 and V/V reference no length, so none of them appears in the importer''s conversion table.","They are value curves rather than index curves, and only the index curve is ever converted.","They were recorded by a metric tool while the depth track alone was recorded by a foot-denominated one."]'::jsonb, 1, 'None of the three units carries a length, so none is in the table and each keeps its converted flag false. Calling them dimensionless is wrong, since GAPI and G/C3 are units of real physical quantities.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 13, 'A file carries a caliper curve declared in IN, a unit that is not in the importer''s conversion table. What happens to it?', '["It passes through unconverted, with its converted flag left false.","It is converted to metres, because the importer recognises inches as a length.","The import stops, because an unrecognised unit on any curve halts the pipeline.","It is dropped from the log list, since a curve in an unhandled unit cannot be registered."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The importer converts what its table recognises rather than everything that arguably carries a length, and only an unrecognised depth unit halts the import. This limitation is one a Professional-tier data manager should be able to state out loud.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 14, 'What does the registration stage of the pipeline do?', '["It converts the curves that prepare left alone, then writes the rows.","It recomputes the start and stop depths from the header before writing them.","It writes the logs and the well header, with validation and identity resolution and no arithmetic.","It rounds the stored values to the precision a report needs before persisting them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each stage adds one kind of value, and register adds identity and persistence. Rounding on the way into storage is the classic error, since it makes a small harmless difference permanent.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-from-qc-to-import', 15, 'Why is a clean foot-denominated file the sharpest test of an import pipeline?', '["Because foot files are rare enough that most pipelines are seldom exercised on them.","Because a foot file usually carries other defects alongside its units.","Because feet cannot be represented exactly in metres, so the conversion is approximate.","Because nothing in it is broken, so the pipeline is translating rather than repairing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A file in feet is foreign rather than defective, and translation is the job this tier exists to teach. The third option inverts the truth, since the factor is exact by definition.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 1, 'What does fixing metres as the internal unit buy every application above the registry?', '["A guarantee that no file will ever arrive denominated in feet.","A depth becomes a plain number, since the unit lives at the boundary and nothing above it carries a unit argument.","A unit column on each well record, which every consumer reads and applies before it computes anything with a depth.","A conversion applied twice, once on write and once on read, so that errors cancel."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With a fixed internal unit two wells can be compared directly, and a grid built from twenty wells does not need to know where any of them came from. Keeping a unit column and converting on read is the arrangement the standard replaces.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 2, 'A project stores each well in whatever unit it arrived in, with a unit column on the well record, and converts inside each application as it reads. Which is a real mechanism by which that produces a wrong depth?', '["The unit column takes storage space that the depth samples need.","Reading a unit column is slower than reading a depth column, so consumers under load skip the check and assume metres.","The conversion factor drifts between releases of the platform, so two consumers reading the same well end up disagreeing.","The unit label is metadata, and metadata gets dropped in an export, a paste, or a join with no column for it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Other real mechanisms are a new consumer written without knowledge of the rule, and a consumer that converts depth correctly while missing a per-length curve. The factor itself never drifts, since it is exact by definition.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 3, 'The factor that turns feet into metres is 0.3048. What kind of number is it?', '["A definition, so the digits stop at the 8 and there is no error bar under it.","A measurement, whose uncertainty is small enough to ignore at the depths a logging tool reaches.","A rounded value of a longer physical constant, kept short at four digits for convenience.","A regional convention that varies with the survey authority under which the well was drilled."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The international foot is defined as exactly 0.3048 metres, so the foot is specified in terms of the metre rather than compared against it. Treating the factor as a rounded measurement is the classic error, and it sends people looking for precision loss where there is none.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 4, 'A converted depth from the pipeline differs from the one you worked out by hand. Why is the conversion factor not a candidate explanation?', '["Because the pipeline and your calculator both round the factor the same way before multiplying by it, so any factor error cancels.","Because the difference is far too large to be explained by a factor that short.","Because the factor is exact, so the arithmetic contributes no error and the difference has to come from somewhere else.","Because the pipeline applies the factor in a different order, which cancels the error."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An exact factor removes itself from the list of suspects, which is what makes the real explanation findable. In this tier''s fixture the difference comes from float32 storage.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 5, 'Why does the importer read the declared unit string instead of inferring the unit from how large the depth values are?', '["Because deep metric wells reach the same magnitudes that shallower wells reach in feet, so the values cannot settle the question.","Because the depth values are held as float32 and cannot be compared against thresholds.","Because the range of the column is known only after the whole file has been parsed.","Because most files declare a unit, so inference would seldom be needed in practice."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A well from 1500 to 1650 is plausible in either unit, and the range heuristic fails hardest on deep wells, which are the wells whose depths carry the most weight. Only the declaration knows.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 6, 'The importer meets a depth unit string it does not recognise. Why is stopping better than defaulting to metres?', '["Because defaulting to metres would break the files that are genuinely in metres.","Because a default would make the import slower on every file that followed.","Because the registry has no way to store a well whose unit is unknown.","Because a foot well relabelled as metres is wrong with no visible symptom, while a stopped import costs one person a few minutes."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Falling back to feet is the same error in the other direction, and a warning in a log file is no better, since warnings are read by whoever is watching at the time while the well outlives them.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 7, 'Where does the pipeline get the start and stop depths it reports for a file?', '["From the STRT and STOP entries in the header, converted by the depth factor.","From the first and last samples of the converted depth column.","From the header for the start, and from the data for the stop.","From the header, with the data used only to check the sample count against it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The header is a claim while the depth column is the fact, so a start read from the data is guaranteed to be a depth at which the well has samples. On feet_20 the two agree, which is a fact about that file rather than a property of the format.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 8, 'You convert the start of feet_20.las by hand and get 4900 x 0.3048 = 1493.52 m. The panel reports 1493.52001953125 m converted, and the tolerance on that reading is 0.01. What is the correct reading of the situation?', '["Your hand answer is wrong, since the pipeline carries the digits your calculator dropped and the graded value is the only correct one.","The pipeline is wrong, since 1493.52 is the exact product and the extra digits are an artefact of display.","Neither can be checked against the other, since one is a decimal value and the other is a stored value.","Both are correct. They agree through the fourth decimal place, both sit far inside the 0.01 tolerance, and both pass."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two describe the same place in the ground to a precision no logging tool can resolve, so the hand answer is a passing answer rather than an approximation to a better one. Treating 1493.52 as wrong is the classic error this lesson exists to prevent.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 9, 'Where do the trailing digits of 1493.52001953125 m converted come from?', '["From the factor, which carries more digits inside the engine than 0.3048.","From an accumulation of rounding error across the 151 samples of the depth column, which grows as the pipeline works its way down the column.","From casting the product to float32, which snaps it to the nearest representable value, since 0.52 has no terminating binary expansion.","From the logging tool itself, which recorded depth to a finer resolution than the header shows and passed the extra digits through to the file."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pipeline multiplies in double precision and casts back to float32 per sample, so there is one rounding at storage rather than a chain of them. Reading the digits as tool resolution is the classic error, since they are a storage artefact and say nothing about the measurement.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 10, 'Why is 4900 ft held in the depth column with no error at all, while its converted value is not?', '["Because the native values are held in double precision, while only the converted values are cast down to float32 before storage.","Because 4900 is a small integer, exactly representable in float32, while the product lands between two representable values.","Because the parser holds native values as text and only converted values become numbers.","Because 4900 had already been rounded to a whole number of feet by the logging tool before it was ever written into the file."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every float32 value is an integer multiplied by a power of two, and small integers are in that set exactly. Nothing has gone wrong until the multiplication.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 11, 'Which statement about the trailing digits of a converted depth is correct?', '["They are exact and reproducible, being the decimal value of one bit pattern.","They vary a little from one machine to another, which is why the reading is graded with a tolerance.","They are measurement noise inherited from the depth reference, the cable and the tool''s own sampling.","They are a rounding error that grows as the pipeline works its way down the depth column."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every float32 value has a terminating decimal expansion, and endings such as 125 or 9375 are the signature of a binary fraction written out in full. Running the import a thousand times gives identical digits on every machine, so treating them as noise sends you hunting a fault that does not exist.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 12, 'Why is float32 an appropriate storage format for a log curve?', '["Because a logging tool records depth to exactly seven significant digits.","Because float64 cannot hold a depth column of this length.","Because seven significant decimal digits is far finer than a wireline depth, and float32 halves the memory of every curve.","Because float32 rounds every stored value toward the value the header declares."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Storage noise thousands of times smaller than the measurement noise contributes nothing to the error budget, and halving the data is a real operational gain. The format is precise enough to be invisible in any petrophysical answer while remaining visible in the last printed digits.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 13, 'What rule does float32 storage impose on any code that works with converted depths?', '["Round every converted depth to two decimal places before comparing.","Never compare converted depths for exact equality. Compare with a tolerance drawn from the physics.","Convert back to the native unit before comparing, since the native values are exact.","Store converted depths as text rather than as numbers, so that the full digits survive every copy and join."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A test for exact equality on a converted depth column would fail on a perfectly regular log, for reasons that have nothing to do with the log. Rounding before comparison makes the harmless difference permanent.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 14, 'A foot-denominated file is imported with the depth conversion skipped, so the native numbers are stored under a metres label. Why does the file''s own QC still pass?', '["Because QC reads the header, and the header was never edited.","Because the sample count and the null counts are unaffected by depth.","Because the error is smaller than the tolerances that the QC checks use.","Because every depth is scaled by the same factor, so the well stays internally self-consistent and only shows up against something outside it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The curves still line up with each other sample for sample and the step is still constant, so nothing inside the file objects. The well is about 3.28 times too deep, since one metre contains 1/0.3048 = 3.2808 feet.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-depth-units', 15, 'Someone converts a foot-denominated depth column by multiplying by 3.2808 instead of by 0.3048. Why is this the least dangerous of the unit errors?', '["Because the result leaves the range of plausible depths, so the number objects to being wrong.","Because 3.2808 and 0.3048 are close enough to each other that the resulting error stays inside the tolerance.","Because the pipeline detects the inverted factor during the factor lookup and refuses to import the file at all.","Because multiplying by 3.2808 gives very nearly the same answer that dividing by 0.3048 would have given."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A wrong number can catch itself only by leaving the plausible range, and that is the mechanism every dangerous unit error lacks. The last option is true as arithmetic and irrelevant here, since dividing by 0.3048 is also the wrong operation for a depth column.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 1, 'When the import pipeline decides whether to convert a curve, what does it actually read?', '["The curve''s mnemonic, which tells the importer what the measurement is.","The unit declared on the depth curve, which sets one unit system for the whole file.","The curve''s unit string, looked up in a fixed conversion table.","The curve''s position in the curve section."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Conversion is keyed on the unit string alone. Assuming the mnemonic decides is the classic error, and it is the reason a curve named SLOWNESS in US/F and a curve named DT in US/M behave differently from what the names suggest.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 2, 'On feet_20, what does the DT row show for the unit before import and the unit after import?', '["US/F before and US/M after.","US/F before and US/F after, because only the depth column is changed by an import.","US/M before and US/M after, because a sonic is always logged in metric units.","US/F before and M after, because every foreign unit in a file becomes metres."]'::jsonb, 0, 'DT arrives in microseconds per foot and leaves in microseconds per metre, and the curve carries the converted flag. Expecting US/F to survive the import is the misconception that only the index column converts.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 3, 'How many curves does the import pipeline unit-convert on feet_20?', '["1, the depth column.","5, because the file is a feet file and everything in it is foreign.","4, because the four measurement curves convert and the index does not.","2, made up of the depth column DEPT and the sonic curve DT."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The graded answer is 2, and the field is tolerance zero, so 1 and 5 both score nothing. Answering 1 comes from treating conversion as a job done to the index column alone.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 4, 'A learner reports that feet_20 had 1 curve unit-converted. Which misconception produced that answer?', '["Confusing curve count with sample count.","Treating unit conversion as something done to the index column alone.","Forgetting that the index curve is excluded from counts.","Reading the count from the well section header rather than from the curve section."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The learner missed DT, whose unit US/F carries a length in the denominator and therefore converts alongside the depth. The index unit is the loudest one in the file because it is declared four times over, which is why it crowds out the curve section on a quick read.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 5, 'A learner reports that feet_20 had 5 curves unit-converted. Which misconception produced that answer?', '["Treating foreignness as a property of the file rather than of each curve''s unit string.","Counting the index curve twice, once as DEPT and once as the depth axis.","Assuming the conversion table holds one entry for every curve in the file.","Believing the sonic must be converted twice, once for time and once for length."]'::jsonb, 0, '5 is the total curve count, so it is really an answer of all of them given without inspecting anything. GR, RHOB and NPHI carry the same units in feet_20 that they carry in the metric files of the teaching set.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 6, 'Why does GR in GAPI pass through the import untouched even though the file it came from is logged in feet?', '["GAPI is a metric unit, so it is already in the form the platform stores.","Gamma ray curves are exempt from conversion because the mnemonic GR appears on a do-not-touch list the importer consults before it ever reads the unit.","GAPI carries no physical dimension at all, being fixed against a calibration standard, so no change of length unit can reach it.","GAPI values are ratios of two like quantities, so the units cancel out."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The API gamma ray unit is defined by an artefact, a calibration pit in which the difference between the low and high activity zones is declared to be 200 API units. A unit defined by a reference sample has nothing in it for a foot to metre conversion to act on.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 7, 'Bulk density in G/C3 does contain a length in its unit. Why is it still not converted?', '["The length is cubed, and the importer only converts units whose length appears to the first power.","The length in it is metric, so there is no foreign length to remove.","Density is a property of a rock sample, and properties of samples are never unit dependent.","The importer converts it to kilograms per cubic metre first, then reports it as unchanged."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The question is never whether a unit has dimensions but whether it contains a length expressed in the foreign system. G/C3 is grams per cubic centimetre, and both parts of it are metric.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 8, 'What makes NPHI in V/V the easiest case in the whole of unit handling?', '["It is already the platform''s internal unit, so the importer finds an entry for it in the conversion table and applies a factor of exactly 1.","Its values are always between zero and one, so any conversion would push them out of range.","The importer treats porosity curves as derived products and leaves derived products alone.","It is dimensionless, because the same length unit appears once above the line and once below it and cancels."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A ratio of two like quantities is the same number whatever unit those quantities were measured in, so there is nothing to convert under any change of unit system. The only thing to watch on a curve like this is whether the file says V/V or a percentage scale, which is a convention question rather than a conversion.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 9, 'In feet_20 the depth column is multiplied by 0.3048 while the sonic is divided by it. What explains the two directions?', '["The sonic is a time and the depth is a length, and times and lengths always convert in opposite directions.","The importer uses a different constant for slowness and expresses it as a division for convenience.","The sonic unit carries its length in the denominator, so quoting the same rock per metre gives a larger number.","The depth column and the sonic use different conversion tables, and the sonic one stores its factor inverted."]'::jsonb, 2, 'US/F is microseconds per foot and US/M is microseconds per metre, and a metre is the longer distance for the wave to cross. A conversion that made the transit time smaller has gone the wrong way, and reaching for the depth factor out of habit is the common slip.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 10, 'A file logged in feet carries a curve named SLOWNESS with the unit US/F. What does the import pipeline do with it?', '["It converts it to US/M, because US/F is in the conversion table and the mnemonic is not consulted.","It leaves it alone, because SLOWNESS is not one of the mnemonics the importer knows.","It refuses the file, because a mnemonic it does not recognise cannot be given a unit.","It converts it to M, because any unit containing F becomes metres."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The conversion decision reads the unit string and nothing else, so an unfamiliar mnemonic carrying a familiar unit converts normally. Expecting the mnemonic to gate the conversion is the classic error this module exists to correct.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 11, 'A metric file carries a curve named DT with the unit US/M. Is it converted, and why?', '["Yes, because DT is a sonic and every sonic curve passes through the conversion table.","No, because US/M is already the internal unit and there is nothing for the conversion to do.","Yes, because the importer normalises every sonic to a single reference length on import.","No, because sonic curves are only converted when the file''s depth column is also converted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Same mnemonic, same measurement, same tool, and a different answer, because the only thing that differed was the unit string. This is the cleanest demonstration that the converted flag tracks the unit rather than the curve identity.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 12, 'A file logged in feet carries a caliper called CALI with the unit IN, for inches. What does the import pipeline do with it?', '["It converts it to M, because inches are a foreign length and foreign lengths are converted.","It converts it to M and records the factor, in the same way it handles the depth column.","It refuses the file, because an unrecognised unit on any curve blocks the import.","It leaves it unconverted, because IN is not one of the units in the conversion table."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The importer converts what its table recognises rather than everything a dimensional argument says it should. Inches are absent from the table, so the curve passes through with its unit and its samples exactly as the parser produced them.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 13, 'Which statement best describes how the importer arrives at a conversion decision?', '["It performs a lookup in a fixed table of unit strings.","It parses the unit into its dimensions and reasons about which of them are foreign.","It compares the curve''s unit against the unit on the depth column and converts any that differ.","It applies a set of rules per curve kind, with one rule for each kind in its vocabulary."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mechanism is a lookup with no inference in it, and the absence of cleverness is deliberate. Anything outside the table is handed to a human rather than guessed at, which is the same policy that makes the parser refuse a depth unit it does not know.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 14, 'What does the pipeline store alongside a curve it has converted?', '["A copy of the original samples, so the conversion can be undone later.","Nothing extra, since the new unit on the curve is a complete record of what happened.","The source unit and the factor used, written into the curve''s provenance.","A checksum of the file, so the conversion can be traced back to its source export."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Conversions either happen visibly or do not happen at all, so an auditor months later can see that this sonic started life in US/F and was multiplied by a known number. Silent unit handling is how a feet well ends up booked as a metres well.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-curve-units', 15, 'Why do unit conversions live in the import layer rather than in the LAS parser?', '["The parser runs before the well section has been read, so at the point where it tokenises the data it does not yet know what units the file declared.","The parser''s contract is fidelity to the file, so that its output can be checked against an independent reader.","Conversions need the depth step, and the step is only computed after parsing finishes.","The parser is shared with other formats that have no units at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Import is where a file stops being a foreign document and becomes a project asset, and turning foreign units into internal ones is exactly what that transition means. Keeping the parser pure is what allows the goldens to pin raw parse fidelity.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 1, 'What is a curve kind?', '["The internal unit the platform stores a curve in once the import has finished.","The importer''s answer to the question of what sort of measurement a curve holds, drawn from a fixed vocabulary.","The vendor''s own classification of the curve, copied out of the curve section description.","A quality grade the importer assigns to a curve after inspecting its samples."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A kind is a short label from a fixed vocabulary, derived from the file rather than from anything a user typed. It says what the measurement is and makes no claim at all about whether the curve is any good.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 2, 'Which input does the importer use to assign a curve''s kind?', '["The unit, since the unit says what physical quantity the curve holds.","The mnemonic and the unit together, with the unit breaking ties.","The curve description in the curve section, falling back to the mnemonic.","The mnemonic alone."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The kind lookup takes the mnemonic, upper-cases it, strips any run suffix after a colon and matches it against fixed name lists. Believing the importer reads the unit to decide the kind is the commonest assumption here, and it is wrong, though the unit is worth reading as corroborating evidence.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 3, 'How many curve kinds does the import pipeline recognise on feet_20?', '["4, one for each of the file''s measurement curves.","5, one for every curve the file declares.","3, the three curves that pass through unconverted.","2, matching the count of curves that were unit-converted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The importer recognises gr, density, neutron and sonic. Answering 5 counts the index curve DEPT, and the field is tolerance zero, so being one out scores nothing.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 4, 'Why is the graded count of recognised kinds on feet_20 4 rather than 5?', '["DEPT is unrecognised, because the importer cannot look up a mnemonic that is also a unit.","One of the five curves carries a kind the platform''s vocabulary has no word for.","DEPT is the index curve, and the count is a count of recognised measurements.","The depth curve is counted separately, under the start and stop depths rather than under kinds."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every well ever imported has exactly one index, so including it would add one to every count and tell you nothing new. Excluding it makes the number answer a real question, which is how many of the file''s measurements the platform understood.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 5, 'Which four kinds does the importer recognise on feet_20?', '["depth, gr, density and neutron.","gr, density, neutron and porosity.","gr, density, sonic and resistivity.","gr, density, neutron and sonic."]'::jsonb, 3, 'They come from GR, RHOB, NPHI and DT in that order. Note that the neutron curve is kind neutron rather than porosity, and that depth belongs to the index and is not part of the count.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 6, 'The kind of DEPT is depth. How does the importer arrive at that?', '["It looks DEPT up in its kind lists, where DEPT sits alongside DEPTH and MD.","It stamps the first curve as depth by position, without looking the mnemonic up.","It reads the unit, sees a recognised depth unit, and infers the kind from it.","It takes the kind from the STRT and STOP entries in the well section."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The index is depth whatever it is called, because its role is structural rather than a matter of what somebody typed in the curve section. The kind lists do hold DEPT, DEPTH and MD, but the first curve never reaches that lookup.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 7, 'Why does a project need kinds at all rather than working directly from mnemonics?', '["Mnemonics are discarded at import, so a kind is the only label the curve keeps.","Kinds are required by the LAS standard, which reserves the curve section for vendor names.","Several vendors spell one measurement several ways, and downstream applications ask for the measurement.","A kind is shorter than a mnemonic, which keeps the registry compact at scale."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One gamma ray arrives as GR, another as SGR, another as CGR. Without a mapping layer each application carries its own list of acceptable spellings, those lists drift apart, and the same well works in one screen and appears to have no gamma ray in the next.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 8, 'What happens to a curve''s original mnemonic once the importer has given it a kind?', '["It is kept and stored with the curve.","It is replaced by the kind, which becomes the curve''s name in the registry.","It is kept only when the kind is null, so that the curve can still be identified.","It is moved into the curve''s provenance and removed from the curve itself."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A kind is added alongside the mnemonic rather than substituted for it. You still see that a curve was called SGR in the file it came from, which matters when you go back to the source or talk to the vendor.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 9, 'A processed file delivers its gamma ray under the mnemonic GR_EDIT. What kind does the importer assign?', '["gr, because the importer strips any suffix after the underscore before looking the name up.","gr, because the lookup matches any mnemonic that begins with a recognised name.","None, because a processed curve is a derived product and derived products are never given kinds.","None, because the lookup is an exact match on the base name and GR_EDIT is not in any list."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The lookup is an exact match rather than a pattern match, and the only decoration it removes is a run suffix after a colon. Assuming a mnemonic that contains a recognised name is enough to guarantee recognition is a common error.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 10, 'A re-logged density arrives as RHOB:2. What kind does the importer assign?', '["None, because RHOB:2 does not appear in any of the kind lists.","density, because the run suffix after the colon is stripped before the lookup.","density, but only after a human confirms the mapping on the import screen.","None, because a colon in a mnemonic makes the curve section line unparseable."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The importer upper-cases the mnemonic and strips anything after a colon, because a run number is not part of the curve''s identity. The base name RHOB is then found in the density list.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 11, 'What does the pipeline do when a curve''s mnemonic appears in none of the kind lists?', '["It imports the curve with a null kind and leaves it out of the recognised count.","It refuses the file, in the same way it refuses a file with an unrecognised depth unit.","It assigns the closest matching kind it can find and marks that assignment as low confidence.","It imports the curve and assigns it the kind of the neighbouring column."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The samples are imported exactly as parsed and the depth axis is still the depth axis, so nothing is wrong beyond the missing label. The curve is complete and queryable, and the only difference is that applications selecting by kind will not find it.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 12, 'Why is a blank kind a better outcome than a confidently wrong one?', '["A blank can be filled in later, whereas a kind is permanent once written to the registry.","A blank costs the importer nothing, whereas a wrong kind takes a second pass to compute.","A blank is visible, because it lowers the recognised count and shows on the mapping.","A blank keeps the curve out of the registry, so nothing downstream can reach it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A wrong kind is silent, because the curve appears everywhere it should not, wearing a label nobody doubts and feeding calculations that have no way to question it. A system that guesses aggressively to avoid blanks trades a visible problem for an invisible one.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 13, 'The importer gives NPHI the kind neutron rather than the kind porosity. Why does that distinction matter?', '["Porosity is not in the platform''s kind vocabulary, so neutron is the nearest available word.","A neutron log is not a porosity until a matrix assumption has been applied to it.","The kind has to match the mnemonic, and NPHI begins with the letter for neutron.","Porosity is a unit rather than a kind, and V/V already records it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The vocabulary names the measurement, which is a neutron response, rather than the petrophysical property somebody derives from it. The importer is careful not to claim a property it has not computed.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 14, 'On feet_20 the sonic is converted from US/F to US/M. What does that conversion do to its kind?', '["It changes the kind from sonic to a metric sonic, which is the internal form.","It confirms the kind, because a successful conversion is what allows the kind to be assigned.","It clears the kind, which a human then reassigns on the import screen.","Nothing, because the kind was read off the mnemonic DT and DT did not change."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Kind and conversion are computed from different inputs, so neither is evidence about the other. The conversion changed the unit column and the converted flag and left the kind column alone.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-curve-kinds', 15, 'In what way is the index curve structurally different from every other curve in a file?', '["It states where each reading was taken, so it is the axis the other curves are sampled against.","It is the only curve the importer converts, since the other curves inherit its unit.","It is the only curve the platform requires, since a file with no measurements is still a log.","It is written by the parser rather than read from the file, since depth is computed from the header."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Strip the gamma ray out of a file and you still have a well log with four curves. Strip the depth column out and you have four unlabelled sequences of numbers with no way to place any of them.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 1, 'When uniformStepM tests a converted depth column, what does it adopt as its candidate step?', '["The mean of every consecutive difference in the column","The STEP value declared in the well section, converted to metres","The first difference, the second sample minus the first","The depth range divided by the number of intervals"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The function takes the first consecutive difference as a candidate and then tries to falsify it against every later difference. Reaching for the mean or the range over the interval count is the averaging error, and reading the header STEP is the trusting error.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 2, 'A later consecutive difference falls outside the tolerance. What does uniformStepM return?', '["Nothing at all, so the step field comes back null","The average of the differences it managed to check","Zero, which the pipeline stores to mark the file as irregular","The difference closest to the candidate, so a step is still reported"]'::jsonb, 0, 'One failing difference rejects the candidate outright and the function returns nothing, which the pipeline stores as a null step. Returning zero is the classic confusion between an absent value and the number 0.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 3, 'What is the tolerance the uniformity test compares each later difference against?', '["0.001 of the candidate step, matching the capstone tolerance","Sixteen float32 epsilons of the maximum absolute depth, on its own","One percent of the average difference in the column","max(0.01 x first, 16 x float32 epsilon x max absolute depth)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tolerance is the larger of a relative term, one percent of the candidate, and a float32 term scaled to the deepest sample. Each term alone is one half of the rule, and the capstone tolerance of 0.001 belongs to the grading, not to the test.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 4, 'For feet_20, which term of the tolerance wins, and what is the resulting allowance?', '["The float32 term, at about 0.003023","The relative term, at about 0.006096","The capstone tolerance, at 0.001","The observed wobble, at about 0.000122"]'::jsonb, 1, 'One percent of the candidate is about 0.006096 and the float32 term is about 0.003023, and the test takes the larger of the two. The other options are the losing term, the grading tolerance and the size of the wobble being tested.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 5, 'feet_20''s converted depth column contains at least two distinct consecutive differences, 0.609619140625 and 0.6094970703125. How does the test treat that?', '["It passes, since about 0.000122 sits well inside the tolerance","It fails, because two distinct differences mean the column is not uniform","It passes, because the test compares only the first and last samples","It fails, and the pipeline falls back to the header STEP converted to metres"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The difference of about 0.000122 is float32 storage rather than anything in the well, and it is about a fiftieth of the allowance of about 0.006096. Expecting exact equality is the error the tolerance exists to prevent.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 6, 'The reported step for feet_20 is 0.609619140625 m converted. What is that number?', '["The mean spacing of the converted column","The nominal 2 foot step multiplied by 0.3048","The smallest consecutive difference found in the column","The first consecutive difference, certified as representative of the rest"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The test returns the candidate it adopted, which is the first difference, once every later difference has agreed with it inside the tolerance. It is one real observed interval rather than any summary of the column.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 7, 'Which of these values is the average step of feet_20''s converted column, taken as the last depth minus the first over the 150 intervals?', '["0.609619140625","0.6094970703125","0.609599609375","0.6096"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The average over the 150 intervals is 0.609599609375. The first option is the value the pipeline reports, the second is another consecutive difference and the last is the exact hand answer of 2 feet times 0.3048.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 8, 'Why is the uniformity check built on a candidate and a falsification rather than on an average?', '["Because averaging every consecutive difference is too slow to run over a long depth column at import","Because an average always returns a number, so the check could never fail","Because the average of a converted column is not defined in float32","Because the header already supplies the average"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dividing the range by the interval count yields a step for any column at all, including a spliced one, so the answer to whether the file is uniform would always be yes. A candidate can be rejected, which is the only reason the check is worth running.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 9, '0.6096, 0.609599609375 and 0.6094970703125 all fall inside the capstone step field''s tolerance of 0.001. What follows?', '["The capstone accepts any of them for that field, while the pipeline still reports the first difference, 0.609619140625","The capstone accepts any of them, so the pipeline has no single reported step","The capstone rejects all three, because only the exact float32 value scores","The tolerance should be read as 0.001 percent of the step"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The wide tolerance keeps the grade from turning on float32 bookkeeping, and it does not change what the pipeline reports, which is specifically the first difference. Concluding that no single value is reported confuses a grading window with the engine result.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 10, 'What does irregular_20''s converted depth column contain?', '["121 samples stepping by about 0.300049 m, 0.5 m and about 0.699951 m","121 samples at a constant 0.5 m spacing, with a single gap near the top of the file","151 samples stepping by about 0.300049 m and 0.5 m","121 samples whose consecutive differences vary only in the fifth decimal place"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The file holds 121 samples and three visibly different spacings, the largest more than twice the smallest. Varying only in the fifth decimal describes feet_20''s float32 wobble instead.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 11, 'On irregular_20 the candidate step is about 0.300049 m. What happens next in the test?', '["The tolerance is about 0.003 m and the next differences miss it by about 0.2 m and 0.4 m","The tolerance is about 0.006096 m, exactly as it was for feet_20, so the next differences pass","The candidate is replaced by the next difference and the walk restarts","The test averages the three spacings across the column and reports the mean of 0.5 m as the step"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The allowance built from the candidate is about 0.003 m and the misses are about a hundred times larger, so the hypothesis is rejected at once. There is no second candidate and no fallback to an average.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 12, 'For irregular_20, what does the pipeline store in the step field, and what does the capstone want in the uniformity field?', '["Both are 0, because the pipeline stores an absent step as zero","Both come back null, and the capstone accepts a blank entry","The step is null and the flag is the integer 0","The step field holds 0.5 and the uniformity flag holds 1"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The test returns nothing, so the step field is null, while the capstone field is a flag graded as 1 for yes and 0 for no. Treating the null step as the number 0, or carrying it into the flag, are two different unit errors.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 13, 'Both feet_20 and irregular_20 have differences that disagree with their candidate. Why does one pass and the other fail?', '["Because feet_20 was converted from feet and irregular_20 was already metric","Because irregular_20 declares a step of 0 in its header","Because feet_20 has more samples, so its disagreements average out","Because of size against the allowance: about 0.000122 m inside about 0.006096 m passes, while about 0.2 m against about 0.003 m fails"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same tolerance rule separates float32 storage noise from a genuine change of sampling by roughly three orders of magnitude. The header and the sample count play no part in the verdict.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 14, 'At what point in the import pipeline does the uniformity test run, and what does that mean for its result?', '["Before conversion, so the returned step must be multiplied by 0.3048","After the depth column is converted, so the returned step is already in metres","In the parser, so the returned step is in the file''s native unit","After the curve kinds are assigned, so the step is reported per kind"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The test receives the converted column, so the step it returns is metric and no second conversion is applied to it. Applying the factor again is a doubling error that produces a step no sample interval in the file matches.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-step-uniformity', 15, 'A delivered log fails the uniformity test. What is the correct response at import?', '["Resample the curves onto an even grid at import so that the well is immediately usable downstream","Copy the header STEP into the step field as a fallback value","Reject the file and ask for a replacement before anything is loaded","Keep the column as logged, record the null step, and decide about resampling later"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pipeline preserves the column, imports the curves and declines to report a step, and the finding goes into the provenance so someone can act on it. Resampling at import is interpretation done silently, and promoting the header replaces a measurement that has already failed with an unverified claim.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 1, 'In the import workflow, where does the step test sit relative to the depth conversion?', '["The step is tested first, so the verdict does not depend on the unit at all","The depth column is converted first, then the column is tested","Both happen in the parser, before the import layer sees the file","The step is read from the header and never tested at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Conversion comes first, so the step the pipeline reports is already metric. Reading the header instead of testing the column is the trusting error this tier is built to remove.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 2, 'How does the importer decide whether a curve needs unit conversion?', '["By asking whether the curve''s kind is one that carries a length","By analysing the unit for a length dimension in the numerator or denominator","By comparing the curve unit against the depth unit in the well section","By an exact lookup of the unit string in a fixed conversion table"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The unit string is trimmed, upper-cased and looked up in a fixed table, with no reasoning about what the unit means. Assuming general dimensional analysis is the error that makes an unconverted length unit look like a bug.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 3, 'Which of these units is in the importer''s conversion table?', '["USEC/FT","IN","G/C3","GAPI"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The whole table is US/F, US/FT, USEC/F and USEC/FT converting to US/M, plus F, FT and FEET converting to M. Inches are absent, and the other two units carry no length at all.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 4, 'A caliper arrives in IN. What does the import pipeline do with it?', '["It converts the curve to metres using the same 0.3048 factor that the depth column used","It refuses the file, because an unconvertible length unit blocks the import","It leaves the curve alone and marks it not converted","It converts the curve and records the factor in the provenance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The table has no entry for inches, so the curve passes through unchanged with its converted flag off. That is the importer working as built, and it is a finding for the reviewer to record rather than a bug to report.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 5, 'What input does the importer use to decide a curve''s kind?', '["The unit string, since kinds and units go together","The curve''s position in the file, for every curve","The curve description text in the curve section","The mnemonic alone, upper-cased and matched against fixed name lists"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Kind comes from the mnemonic with any run suffix after a colon stripped, and the unit is never consulted. That makes the kind column and the converted column independent findings during QC.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 6, 'Why is the graded count of curve kinds recognised on feet_20 4 rather than 5?', '["Because one of the five curves has a mnemonic no list matches","Because the index curve is stamped depth by position and excluded from the count","Because the sonic is counted under its converted unit instead","Because a curve can only be counted as a kind once its unit has been converted to the project standard"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The file has five curves, and the four counted kinds are gr, density, neutron and sonic. DEPT is the index curve, special-cased by position rather than by lookup, and left out of the count, so 5 is the classic overcount.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 7, 'Which two curves are the 2 curves unit-converted on feet_20?', '["GR and DT","DEPT and RHOB","DEPT, the index curve, and DT","DT and NPHI"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The depth column converts from F to M and the sonic converts from US/F to US/M, because both unit strings are in the conversion table. GR, RHOB and NPHI have no table entry and pass through untouched.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 8, 'A reviewer signs off feet_20 with 1 curve unit-converted. What has gone wrong?', '["The sonic in US/F was missed, so a transit time in feet will be read as metric","The depth column was counted twice in the earlier check","The index curve should be excluded from the converted count as well as the kind count","Nothing, because the count excludes the index curve"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only the depth column was counted, and DT in US/F was left out, which is the assumption that a file is either a feet file or a metres file. The count field carries a tolerance of 0, so a value of 1 scores nothing.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 9, 'During QC, what suggests that a step field was copied from the header rather than measured?', '["A step reported to twelve decimal places in the registry","A step of 0.6096 exactly, or a step of 2","A step field that is null on an irregular file","A step that differs from the average of the column"]'::jsonb, 1, 'feet_20 declares a step of 2 in feet, so a stored 0.6096 or 2 points to a converted header claim rather than a measured column. A null step on an irregular file and a step that differs from the average are both the pipeline behaving correctly.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 10, 'What does QC check 5 require of every depth that came out of a conversion?', '["That it is rounded to the precision a reader needs","That it is quoted in both the file''s native unit and in metres wherever it appears","That it is recomputed by hand before publication","That it is labelled as converted, in metres, with its source unit recoverable"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A converted depth quoted with no note of its origin cannot be audited later, while one labelled as converted from 4900 F at 0.3048 can be re-derived by anybody. The label is what turns a later dispute into a question about the factor.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 11, 'What is the graded start depth for feet_20, and what tolerance does the capstone allow?', '["4900, with a tolerance of 0.01","1493.52, with a tolerance of 0.001","1493.52001953125 m converted, with a tolerance of 0.01","1584.9599609375 m converted, with a tolerance of 0.01, read from the stop tile"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The field is the converted start depth in m and the tolerance is 0.01. Reporting the native 4900 in feet is the unit error, and 1584.9599609375 m converted is the stop depth field.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 12, 'Which capstone fields carry a tolerance of 0?', '["The two depth fields and the step field","The step field and the two counts","The two counts and the uniformity flag","Every field, since the pipeline is deterministic"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Curves unit-converted, curve kinds recognised and the uniformity flag are graded exactly, so 2, 4 and 0 are the only values that score. Assuming a count has a tolerance is the error that makes a near miss feel survivable.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 13, 'A learner computes 4900 times 0.3048 as 1493.52 and 5200 times 0.3048 as 1584.96 and enters both. What happens?', '["Both fail, because the graded values are the float32 representations","Both pass, because each sits far inside the 0.01 tolerance","The start passes and the stop fails","Both fail, because the capstone requires the panel readings"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The graded values are 1493.52001953125 m converted and 1584.9599609375 m converted, and the hand answers differ from them by far less than 0.01. The hand arithmetic is the same depth at the precision a person needs.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 14, 'How should the uniformity result for irregular_20 be entered on the capstone?', '["As the word no, written out","As the integer 0","As null, since no step exists","As 0.5, the file''s average spacing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The field is graded as 1 for yes and 0 for no, so the entry is the integer 0. Writing a word, or carrying the pipeline''s null step into a field that wants a flag, are both units errors.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-import-workflow', 15, 'A sonic left in US/F reaches a petrophysicist whose Wyllie equation expects US/M. What is the result?', '["A porosity that is wrong and plausible at the same time, with nothing in the output saying so","An error, because the equation rejects the unit","A porosity that is correct, since the units cancel","A depth shift of about a third in the computed curve"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing checks the unit downstream, so the arithmetic runs and returns a reasonable looking number from a transit time in the wrong denominator. This is why the converted count of 2 rather than 1 matters at import.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'How do the three Well Data Manager tiers divide the work?', '["The Associate tier reads headers, this tier reads data, and the Expert tier writes to the registry.","The Associate tier handles metric files, this tier handles foot files, and the Expert tier handles archives.","All three tiers import single files, with each tier adding more curves to the import.","The Associate tier judges one file, this tier imports one file, and the Expert tier audits a delivery."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The progression is about what is being decided rather than about which files are handled. Adding curves up across files belongs to the Expert tier and not to this one.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'What does a converted curve carry alongside its samples so that the conversion can be audited later?', '["A checksum of the original file, so that the source can be located again.","The unit it was converted from, the unit it was converted to, and the factor applied.","The name of the person who approved the import at the time.","A copy of the native samples alongside, so that the conversion can be undone by subtraction later."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A converted number with no record of the conversion is a number nobody can audit, while one carrying its source unit and factor can be checked, reversed and explained. Provenance is the defence that survives you.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'Three of this tier''s six graded readings carry no tolerance at all. Why are those three graded exactly?', '["Because they are the readings that the panel happens to display to fewer decimal places than the other three.","Because they are taken from the header rather than from the data, so no storage error can ever reach any of them.","Because they are counts and a verdict, so an answer one off comes from a wrong model rather than a slip.","Because their tolerance was set to zero in order to keep the capstone difficult."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Getting the converted count wrong means believing unit trouble is a depth problem, and getting the kind count wrong means counting the index curve as a measurement. Neither is arithmetic, so neither gets a tolerance.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'The native span of feet_20.las is 300 ft. Converting the span gives 300 x 0.3048 = 91.44 m, and subtracting the two hand-converted end depths gives 91.44 m as well. Why must the two routes agree?', '["Because multiplication by a constant is linear, so converting then subtracting and subtracting then converting give the same result.","Because the tolerance on each end depth is wide enough to absorb the difference.","Because the rounding at the two ends happens to cancel on this file.","Because the header and the data agree with each other on this particular file."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The agreement is a property of the arithmetic rather than of the file, so a disagreement between the two routes means an arithmetic slip. That makes the span a cheap closure check on any conversion you do.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'An importer converts the depth column of feet_20.las correctly and leaves the sonic in US/F. What does the converted-curve count read, and what does the miss mean?', '["It reads 2, and the miss shows up somewhere else instead, as an unrecognised curve kind on the sonic.","It reads 0, since a conversion that is only partly applied is not recorded by the pipeline at all.","It reads 1 instead of 2, since the model of unit trouble behind it stops at the depth column.","It reads 2, since the count records the curves the importer inspected rather than the ones it changed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The graded count is 2, made up of DEPT and DT. Left unconverted a transit time in microseconds per foot still looks like a transit time, so anything computed from that curve carries the factor forward quietly.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'You are writing a memo about feet_20.las and also loading the file into the registry. What may be rounded and what may not?', '["Round in both places, so that the memo and the registry agree with each other.","Round in neither place, since a converted depth must always be quoted in full.","Round in the registry to keep the stored values tidy, and quote full precision in the memo.","Round in the memo to the precision your reader needs, and store what the pipeline stored."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Rounding on the way into storage is how a small harmless difference becomes a permanent one, while rounding on the way into a sentence for a person costs nothing and reads better. The third option inverts the rule.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'The converted start of feet_20.las is stored above the exact decimal 1493.52 and the converted stop is stored below the exact decimal 1584.96. What does that pattern show?', '["That the pipeline rounds up at the start of a column and down at the end of it.","That storage rounds to the nearest representable value, which can lie on either side.","That the two ends were converted with slightly different factors.","That the error accumulates down the column and changes sign somewhere near the midpoint."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One value rounds up and one rounds down, which is what rounding to nearest looks like when you see two examples of it. There is no accumulation, since each sample is converted and cast on its own.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'Which is the correct way to hand a colleague the start depth of feet_20.las?', '["1493.52001953125 m, converted from 4900 ft.","1493.52001953125, which is the start depth of the well.","4900, since the native value is the one the file actually contains.","About 1493.5, since anything finer is beyond what the tool resolved anyway."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A converted depth is derived, so it needs its unit and the word converted, and naming the native value lets the reader check which factor was applied. The last option rounds fairly for a memo but drops both the unit and the conversion.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'Why does the decimal expansion of 1493.52001953125 terminate rather than running on forever?', '["Because the pipeline truncates the expansion at seventeen digits for display.","Because the conversion factor terminates, so the product must terminate too.","Because float32 rounds every stored value to a terminating decimal before writing it.","Because every float32 value is an integer multiplied by a power of two, and such a value always has a terminating decimal expansion."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Endings such as 5, 25, 125 or 9375 are the signature of a binary fraction written out in full. The expansion is long rather than infinite, which is why the printed digits look surprising and are nevertheless exact.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'A well enters the registry about 3.28 times too deep because its foot-denominated depth column was never converted. Which pairing of symptom and first misdiagnosis is the realistic one?', '["The null counts look wrong, and the file is blamed for carrying a bad null flag in its well section.","The curve names look wrong, and the vendor is blamed for a bad export.","A top on that well misses its neighbours by hundreds of metres, and a bad pick is blamed.","The sample count looks wrong, and the parser gets blamed for dropping rows out of the data block."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The depth error leaves everything inside the file intact, so every symptom appears in comparisons against other wells. A gridded surface with a spurious low blamed on geology, and a well tie that will not tie blamed on the seismic, are the other realistic pairings.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'This tier suggests a particular way of working with the import panel. What is it?', '["Predict each reading from the rules, write it down, then open the panel and compare.","Open the panel first, then reason backwards from its readings to the rules behind them.","Use the panel only on those files that have already failed a QC check somewhere upstream.","Run the panel on each file twice and compare the two runs against each other for stability."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A prediction that matches confirms the rule, and a prediction that misses tells you which rule you have wrong. The last option would find nothing, since the pipeline is deterministic and gives identical digits on every run.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'Which statement about the foot used by the importer is correct?', '["The importer chooses between the international foot and the older survey foot from the file''s location.","The importer uses the international foot for every foot-flavoured unit string it recognises.","The two historic definitions of the foot are identical, so the choice never arises in practice.","The older survey definition is the one that matters at logging depths."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A second definition exists in older United States survey practice and differs by a tiny amount that is not zero, which matters in land surveying over long distances rather than at logging depths. A foot is a defined quantity, so know which definition you have when precision reaches that level.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'The importer proposes a well name, a unique well identifier, a kelly bushing elevation and a total depth. Why does a person confirm them before they are written?', '["Because the pipeline cannot read those entries until the data section has been registered.","Because confirming them is what triggers the unit conversion for the elevation and the total depth.","Because the registry rejects any well header written without an operator signature on it.","Because the entries are a suggestion read from header sections the format does not police, and surface coordinates are absent from most LAS files and stay manual."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The elevation and total depth are converted to metres where their declared units say feet, and pass through with a note where the unit is unrecognised, so the pipeline proposes rather than asserts. Identity is the one thing the file cannot establish on its own.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'Which set correctly lists the importer''s defences against a unit error?', '["Read the declared unit, look the factor up and refuse when the lookup fails, convert every curve on its own unit, and record the source unit and factor.","Infer the unit from the depth range, default to metres when unsure, convert the depth column, and write a warning to the log.","Read the declared unit, convert the depth column, round the result, and keep the native values alongside it.","Ask the user for the unit, convert on read inside each application, and keep a unit column on the well record."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The first three defences prevent the mistake at import, while the fourth, provenance, is what lets somebody find it if it happens anyway. Defaulting to metres and converting on read are each the arrangement this module argues against.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'Which pair of statements correctly describes the two decisions the importer makes about a curve?', '["The kind comes from the unit, and the conversion comes from the mnemonic.","Both decisions read the mnemonic and the unit together, weighing one against the other.","Both decisions read the unit, with the mnemonic used only when the unit is missing.","The kind comes from the mnemonic alone, and the conversion comes from the unit string alone."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Two separate questions, two separate inputs, two separate answers, computed independently on the same curve. Believing the importer reads the unit to decide the kind is what most people assume, and it is the reason the two counts in this course are so often confused.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'A curve arrives as RHOB carrying the unit US/F. What does the import pipeline do?', '["It gives it the kind sonic, because the unit says microseconds per foot.","It gives it the kind density from the mnemonic and converts it to US/M from the unit.","It gives it the kind density and leaves the unit alone, because density curves do not convert.","It refuses the curve, because the mnemonic and the unit describe different measurements."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two decisions are independent, so a curve can get its kind from one half of its declaration and its conversion from the other. A mismatch of this sort is not blocked by the importer, and it is exactly the case where a human should read the file before trusting the curve.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'Which pair of counts does the pipeline report for feet_20?', '["1 curve unit-converted and 4 curve kinds recognised.","2 curves unit-converted and 5 curve kinds recognised, counting the index.","2 curves unit-converted and 4 curve kinds recognised.","4 curves unit-converted and 2 curve kinds recognised, with the two counts the other way round."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both are tolerance zero. The 2 is DEPT and DT, and the 4 excludes the index curve, so the two numbers count different populations of the same five rows.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'A feet file carries a sonic under the mnemonic DTCO with the unit USEC/FT. What does the pipeline report for it?', '["Kind sonic, and the curve is converted.","Kind sonic, and the curve is not converted, because USEC/FT is a spelling the table does not carry.","No kind, and the curve is converted, because DTCO is not a mnemonic the importer knows.","No kind and no conversion, since neither the mnemonic nor the unit is one the platform uses."]'::jsonb, 0, 'DTCO is in the sonic list, and USEC/FT is another spelling of microseconds per foot that the conversion table does carry. Both decisions succeed, and each one succeeded on its own input.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'A feet file carries a curve called TVD with the unit FT. What does the pipeline report for it?', '["It converts the curve and gives it the kind depth, since TVD is a depth.","It converts the curve, and the kind is null.","It leaves the curve unconverted and gives it the kind depth.","It leaves the curve unconverted, and the kind is null."]'::jsonb, 1, 'FT is in the conversion table, so the curve converts. TVD is in none of the kind lists and it is not the index curve, so no kind is assigned, and the curve is a converted measurement that nothing selecting by kind will find.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'A feet file carries a curve called SLOWNESS with the unit US/F and a caliper called CALI with the unit IN. What does the pipeline report for the two of them?', '["Both convert and both carry a kind, since both units are foreign.","SLOWNESS is given the kind sonic and CALI is converted to M, because the importer works out what each curve plainly measures.","Neither converts and neither carries a kind, since neither mnemonic is listed.","SLOWNESS is converted to US/M and given no kind, while CALI keeps its unit IN and is given the kind caliper."]'::jsonb, 3, 'US/F is in the conversion table while SLOWNESS is in none of the kind lists, so that curve converts and gets no kind. CALI is in the caliper list while IN is absent from the conversion table, so that curve keeps its unit and does get a kind. One decision is answered from the unit and the other from the mnemonic, and the pair shows the split by coming out opposite ways.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'The count of unit-converted curves includes the index curve while the count of recognised kinds excludes it. What justifies treating them differently?', '["The index really is converted, whereas its kind is stamped by position and nothing could have failed.","The two counts are produced by different parts of the pipeline and were never meant to agree.","The index is the only curve whose conversion a user can see, so it has to be counted somewhere.","Counts of conversions are per file while counts of kinds are per curve, so the totals differ."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The conversion count measures work actually done, and the depth column was genuinely multiplied by 0.3048. The kind count measures how well the platform''s vocabulary matched the file, and the index contributes no information to that because it would be depth regardless of what the file said.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'On feet_20, which curve appears both among the converted curves and among the recognised kinds?', '["DEPT.","RHOB.","DT.","GR."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The converted rows are DEPT and DT, and the counted kinds are gr, density, neutron and sonic. DT is the only row in both populations, and DEPT is in the first and not the second because it is the index.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'Both capstone counts are graded with a tolerance of zero. What does that mean in practice?', '["An answer within one of the graded value earns partial credit.","The counts are rounded to the nearest whole number before comparison.","The counts are checked only when the depth values already agree.","An answer that is one out scores nothing at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'There is no tolerance to hide in on either field. This is why the method matters more than the memory: inspect the units to reach 2, and exclude the index to reach 4.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'A file''s first curve carries the unit GAPI. What does the import pipeline do?', '["It imports the file and marks the depth axis as unknown for a human to fix.","It refuses the import, because the index unit is not a recognised depth unit.","It looks at the second curve instead and uses that as the index.","It imports the file and treats the depth values as metres by default."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The importer takes the first curve as the index whatever it is called, and an unrecognised index unit makes every reading in the file unplaceable, so there is nothing left worth importing. This is why an unrecognised depth unit is a refusal while an unrecognised mnemonic is only a warning.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'A project curve is labelled US/M and its values are about a third of what neighbouring wells show at the same formation. What is the likely explanation?', '["It is an unconverted per-foot slowness that was relabelled rather than converted.","It was converted twice, once at export and once at import.","It was logged with a different tool whose reference length is shorter than a metre.","It is a genuine reading from a faster formation and needs no action."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A per-foot slowness quoted per metre should be the larger number, so a curve wearing US/M with per-foot values has had its label changed without its samples being touched. The fix belongs in the source file rather than in the project.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'The platform stores density in G/C3 rather than in kilograms per cubic metre. What does that tell you about what the import layer is for?', '["Its job is to store every curve in the unit the vendor supplied, whatever that unit is.","Its job is to convert every curve to the SI coherent unit for its quantity.","Its job is to remove foreign-system length references, not to impose a textbook unit system.","Its job is to leave metric units alone and to convert only the curves that feed downstream calculations."]'::jsonb, 2, 'G/C3 is not the SI coherent unit for density, and the importer normalises it to nothing. Converting only what must be converted is a feature, because every conversion is a chance to be wrong.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'A file imports cleanly with an index and six measurements, and the pipeline reports four recognised kinds. What do you know?', '["Two measurements carry no kind, and nothing in the count says which two or why.","Two measurements failed to import and are missing from the registry.","Three measurements carry no kind, since the index is one of the six.","Two measurements were imported with a low confidence kind that needs confirming."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Six measurements less four recognised leaves two blanks, and the count is the quickest way to see that there are any. Finding which two, and reading each one''s description, unit and first few values, is the next move at the import screen.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'You are handed a thirty curve section from a feet file and asked for the conversion count. What is the reliable method?', '["Count the curves, then subtract the ones whose mnemonics the platform does not know.","Count the curves that a downstream application would need, since only those are converted.","Take the depth unit from the well section and apply it to every curve in the file.","Work down the unit column writing yes or no, then count the yeses."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The count falls out of an inspection of units rather than out of reasoning about curves, and a thirty curve file is thirty independent yes or no decisions rather than thirty times the work. Most logging units are dimensionless, defined against a calibration standard, or already metric, so most of the answers are no.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'Why does the value 0.6094970703125 appear among feet_20''s consecutive differences at all?', '["Because the logging tool changed its sample rate partway down the well, which the header does not record","Because the header STEP of 2 was applied to only part of the column","Because the converted depths are stored as float32, and each rounding carries into the gap","Because the average step of the file is close to that value"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing in the borehole changed between those two samples, and the spread of about 0.000122 is an artefact of 32-bit storage. Reading it as an acquisition change is the error the tolerance was written to absorb.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'A registry step field for feet_20 holds 0.6096 exactly. What should a reviewer suspect?', '["That the header claim of 2 feet was converted and stored rather than the column measured","That the column was measured and the value rounded for display","That the file was resampled during import","That the sonic conversion was applied to the depth column"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The measured value from the converted column is 0.609619140625, so a stored 0.6096 points at the header rather than at a measurement. Trusting the header STEP is the failure the step check exists to catch.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'Which change would make the pipeline report a step for irregular_20?', '["Widening the float32 term of the tolerance so that deep samples are forgiven more","Converting its depth column back to feet before the test is run","Reading the header, which declares a step of 0","Replacing the test with an average of the differences"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An average returns a number for any column at all, so a check built on one can never reject a file. Widening the float32 term does not reach misses of about 0.2 m and about 0.4 m, and the header declares 0 for exactly this kind of file.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'A well that was already in metres has been converted anyway, and its frame now reads about a third of its true depth. Which QC check catches this?', '["Check 4, because the converted column would then fail the uniformity test","Check 1, from the native and converted frames","Check 5, because the depths would not be labelled as converted","Check 2, by counting how many curves came out converted"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first check quotes the native unit, the factor and the converted frame together, so the mistake is visible at a glance. The step test would still pass, because a uniformly sampled column stays uniform when every sample is scaled by the same factor.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'What is the second pass of QC check 2 looking for?', '["A curve carrying a length unit that the conversion table does not cover","Curves whose kind was not recognised","Curves that the pipeline converted twice","A depth column left in its native unit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The first pass confirms that every curve whose unit is in the table converted, and the second reads the units of those that did not, because the importer converts what it recognises rather than everything it should. A caliper in IN is the standing case and is a finding to record.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'Five of the six capstone fields are read on feet_20. Which one is not?', '["The count of curves unit-converted, which needs the campaign summary","The converted depth step","The uniformity field, which is read on irregular_20","The converted start depth"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The uniformity field asks whether irregular_20 has a uniform step, so the panel has to be switched to that file, where the answer is the integer 0. The import panel handles one teaching file at a time and produces no campaign totals.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'Which response to an irregular log is never legitimate?', '["Resampling later as an explicit, recorded step with the original kept","Copying the header STEP into the step field","Using the curves as they are","Asking the sender whether the export itself caused the irregularity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The header claim has already been contradicted by the column, so storing it overrides a measurement with an assertion in a field that keeps no record of its origin. The other three are all defensible as long as the choice is recorded.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Which capstone fields are the ones that tolerate 0.01?', '["The converted stop depth together with the converted step","The converted step and the uniformity flag","The two count fields for curves and kinds","The two converted depth fields"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The converted start and stop depths are graded in m with a tolerance of 0.01, which is why the hand answers of 1493.52 and 1584.96 both pass. The step field allows 0.001 and the counts and the flag allow nothing.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'Why does the importer treat DEPT differently from the other four curves on feet_20?', '["Because it is the index curve, stamped depth by its position and left out of the kind count","Because DEPT is the only mnemonic that appears in the kind lists","Because its unit is the only one in the conversion table","Because the parser reports it separately"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The first curve is stamped depth because of where it sits rather than by any lookup, and the graded kind count excludes it, giving 4 from five curves. Counting it produces the classic 5.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'An application wants to turn a sample count into a thickness on irregular_20. What does the registry give it, and what should it do?', '["A step of 0.5 m, the file''s average spacing, so it multiplies by that","A step of 0, so it multiplies and gets zero","A null step, so it must not assume any spacing","The header STEP, converted to metres"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The uniformity test returned nothing, so the step field is null and the file offers no single spacing to multiply by. Treating that null as the number 0, or reaching for the header, are the two ways this goes wrong quietly.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'Which panel reading lets you check the uniformity verdict for yourself rather than taking it on trust?', '["The sample count shown next to the native and converted frames","The first differences shown beside the reported step","The native depth unit together with the factor to metres","The per-curve converted flags"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Seeing the first differences next to the reported step shows both the candidate and the wobble the tolerance is absorbing. On irregular_20 the same tile shows differences of visibly different sizes and a verdict of 0.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'Which statement correctly separates how kind and conversion are decided?', '["Both are decided from the unit string, which is why they agree","Both come from the mnemonic, with the unit used for display","Kind comes from the unit and conversion from the mnemonic","Kind comes from the mnemonic alone and conversion from the unit string alone"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The kind lookup never consults the unit and the conversion lookup never consults the mnemonic, so the two columns are independent findings during QC. Expecting them to agree is what makes an unconverted curve with a recognised kind look like a contradiction.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'Why does the uniformity tolerance include a term of 16 float32 epsilons times the maximum absolute depth?', '["Because sixteen samples are checked before the candidate is accepted at import","Because the depth column is stored in metres","Because float32 values are spaced further apart as they get larger","Because the capstone grades the step to 0.001"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The floor follows the arithmetic rather than the geology, so a fine step at great depth is not rejected for storage noise alone. On feet_20 that term is about 0.003023 and loses to the relative term of about 0.006096.'),
  ('welldata', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'Why is quietly resampling an irregular log at import the wrong response?', '["Because it is interpretation, and it destroys evidence","Because resampling always produces a coarser grid than the original","Because the pipeline cannot resample a float32 column safely","Because the header STEP would then disagree with the data"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Choosing a spacing and an interpolation changes the data, and once the even frame is stored nobody downstream can tell the file was ever irregular. The right response keeps the column as logged, records the null step, and leaves the choice to an explicit later stage.');
